The Pursued — Temporary Startup Implementation Plan

1. Purpose

1. This temporary document tracks implementation from app launch to the main menu's "انطلاق" button.
2. It prevents forgotten requirements, conflicting files, and unnecessary scope expansion.
3. It is an implementation checklist, not a replacement for the game's design documents.
4. This document may be updated when the current scope is completed and the next scope is approved.

2. Mandatory Project Rules

5. Before every file change, reread this document and inspect the live GitHub repository on "main".
6. Review all six existing design documents and the relevant code/configuration files before making decisions.
7. Preserve "README.md", "README 2", "README 3", "README4", "README 5", and "README 6" unchanged.
8. Follow the existing Godot 4 project structure and avoid duplicate files or conflicting implementations.
9. Change or create one file at a time, provide its complete contents, and wait for user confirmation.
10. Never claim a build or runtime test succeeded unless its result has been verified.
11. Do not expand into full gameplay, world maps, enemy systems, or unrelated features during this scope.

3. Required Startup Flow

12. Launch the app into a full-screen splash screen displaying the game logo.
13. The final logo image will be supplied by the user later; do not assume it already exists.
14. On first launch, present a choice between lightweight resources and high-quality resources.
15. Install or obtain the essential resources required to proceed.
16. Obtain the resource pack matching the user's selected quality level.
17. Save the selected setup so the choice is not repeated on every launch.
18. On later launches, show a progress indicator while startup checks and required preparation run.
19. Skip first-launch selection when a valid saved choice already exists.
20. Detect missing or invalid required resources and provide a recovery path.
21. Do not claim real downloadable resource packs exist until their files and hosting are verified.
22. After successful preparation, open the main menu.
23. Show a 3D player character in the main menu with a suitable animation or motion.
24. Provide the "انطلاق" button and placeholders for general settings and player settings.
25. Stop this scope at the main menu; the "انطلاق" destination must not imply completed gameplay.

4. Initial File Checklist

26. "project.godot" — modify startup configuration and main-scene entry only as needed.
27. "scenes/main.tscn" — inspect and adapt the existing scene without losing reusable content.
28. "scripts/player_controller.gd" — inspect dependencies before any necessary compatibility changes.
29. "scenes/splash_screen.tscn" — create the splash-screen scene.
30. "scripts/splash_screen.gd" — implement splash behavior and transition.
31. "scenes/resource_setup.tscn" — create the first-launch resource-choice interface.
32. "scripts/resource_setup.gd" — handle the selection and setup flow.
33. "scripts/resource_manager.gd" — manage saved selection, validation, progress, and recovery.
34. "scenes/main_menu.tscn" — create the main-menu scene and 3D character presentation.
35. "scripts/main_menu.gd" — handle menu buttons and the approved next transition.

5. Verification and Completion

36. The initial estimate is ten project code, scene, and configuration files.
37. Revise this count only when repository inspection proves an additional file is necessary.
38. The logo image, actual resource packs, and download hosting are outside that ten-file count.
39. Verify scene paths, script attachments, resource references, and startup transitions.
40. Check first-launch selection, later-launch behavior, progress display, and missing-resource recovery.
41. Verify that the main menu is reachable and its buttons behave as documented.
42. Record any unverified behavior honestly; do not present a planned feature as tested.
43. Do not request APK installation at this stage.
44. APK testing remains deferred until the agreed playable prototype is ready.
45. After this scope is completed and verified, stop and ask the user to choose the next development scope.

6. Temporary Document Lifecycle

46. Consult this checklist before every implementation step.
47. Update it when a requirement is completed, changed, or found unnecessary.
48. Before starting another scope, inspect the repository and existing design documents again.
49. Add the next approved file list to this document instead of guessing future files in advance.
50. Keep this document temporary and remove or archive it only after the user approves doing so.
